1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,329
In this final pre-calculus unit, we'll
meet the idea that starts calculus: the

2
00:00:05,329 --> 00:00:05,739
limit.

3
00:00:06,867 --> 00:00:13,732
The limit of f of x as x approaches a is
the value f of x gets close to, as x gets

4
00:00:13,732 --> 00:00:14,653
close to a.

5
00:00:15,103 --> 00:00:19,349
What happens exactly at a doesn't matter,
only what happens nearby.

6
00:00:20,467 --> 00:00:27,043
Take f of x equals x squared minus one
over x minus one. It's undefined at one.

7
00:00:27,043 --> 00:00:28,125
Make a table.

8
00:00:28,575 --> 00:00:34,482
At point nine, one point nine. At point
nine nine, one point nine nine. At one

9
00:00:34,482 --> 00:00:38,495
point zero one, two point zero one. The
limit is two.

10
00:00:39,633 --> 00:00:44,647
On a graph, trace toward x equals a from
both sides and see where the heights head.

11
00:00:45,097 --> 00:00:50,410
A hole at one, two still has limit two,
even if f of one is something else, or

12
00:00:50,410 --> 00:00:51,091
undefined.

13
00:00:52,233 --> 00:00:58,450
A one-sided limit approaches from just one
direction. The left limit uses x less than

14
00:00:58,450 --> 00:01:01,449
a; the right limit uses x greater than a.

15
00:01:01,899 --> 00:01:06,443
The two-sided limit exists only if both
one-sided limits agree.

16
00:01:07,567 --> 00:01:12,396
For a jump, like a step function, the left
and right limits differ, so the limit

17
00:01:12,396 --> 00:01:13,241
doesn't exist.

18
00:01:13,691 --> 00:01:19,647
One over x squared near zero grows without
bound, so that limit doesn't exist either;

19
00:01:19,647 --> 00:01:21,329
we write it as infinity.

20
00:01:22,467 --> 00:01:27,240
Your turn. If the limit from the left is
three and the limit from the right is

21
00:01:27,240 --> 00:01:29,933
three, but f of a is five, what's the
limit?

22
00:01:30,383 --> 00:01:31,919
Pause the video and try it.

23
00:01:32,369 --> 00:01:35,996
The limit is three. The function's value
at a doesn't affect it.

24
00:01:37,133 --> 00:01:42,104
In this lesson, we'll compute limits
exactly with algebra instead of tables.

25
00:01:43,233 --> 00:01:46,988
For polynomials and most nice functions,
just substitute.

26
00:01:47,438 --> 00:01:53,296
The limit as x approaches two of x squared
plus three x is four plus six, which is

27
00:01:53,296 --> 00:01:53,582
ten.

28
00:01:54,700 --> 00:02:01,006
If substitution gives zero over zero,
simplify first. The limit as x approaches

29
00:02:01,006 --> 00:02:04,918
three of x squared minus nine over x minus
three.

30
00:02:05,368 --> 00:02:10,702
Factor to x plus three times x minus
three, cancel, and substitute: six.

31
00:02:11,833 --> 00:02:17,526
With square roots, multiply by the
conjugate. The limit as x approaches zero

32
00:02:17,526 --> 00:02:21,796
of the square root of x plus four, minus
two, all over x.

33
00:02:22,246 --> 00:02:28,465
Multiply top and bottom by the square root
of x plus four plus two. The top becomes

34
00:02:28,465 --> 00:02:31,462
x, which cancels, leaving one over four.

35
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:39,313
With complex fractions, combine first. The
limit as x approaches zero of one over x

36
00:02:39,313 --> 00:02:42,306
plus two, minus one half, all over x.

37
00:02:42,756 --> 00:02:49,310
The top becomes negative x over two times
x plus two. Divide by x: negative one over

38
00:02:49,310 --> 00:02:53,914
two times x plus two, which approaches
negative one fourth.

39
00:02:55,033 --> 00:02:58,596
For a piecewise function, check each side
with its own rule.

40
00:02:59,046 --> 00:03:05,065
If f is x plus one for x less than two and
x squared minus one for x at least two,

41
00:03:05,065 --> 00:03:08,368
both sides give three, so the limit is
three.

42
00:03:09,500 --> 00:03:15,530
Your turn. Find the limit as x approaches
negative one of x squared plus three x

43
00:03:15,530 --> 00:03:17,414
plus two over x plus one.

44
00:03:17,864 --> 00:03:19,400
Pause the video and try it.

45
00:03:19,850 --> 00:03:24,928
Factor: x plus one times x plus two.
Cancel, and substitute: one.

46
00:03:26,066 --> 00:03:31,101
In this lesson, we'll ask what happens to
a function as x grows without bound.

47
00:03:32,233 --> 00:03:36,478
The limit as x approaches infinity of one
over x is zero.

48
00:03:36,928 --> 00:03:39,744
As x gets huge, one over x gets tiny.

49
00:03:40,866 --> 00:03:45,951
For rational functions, divide every term
by the highest power of x in the

50
00:03:45,951 --> 00:03:46,775
denominator.

51
00:03:47,225 --> 00:03:53,132
Three x squared plus x over two x squared
minus five: divide by x squared to get

52
00:03:53,132 --> 00:03:59,039
three plus one over x, over two minus five
over x squared. That approaches three

53
00:03:59,039 --> 00:03:59,556
halves.

54
00:04:00,699 --> 00:04:05,863
Bottom degree bigger: the limit is zero.
Same degree: the ratio of leading

55
00:04:05,863 --> 00:04:09,979
coefficients. Top bigger: the function
grows without bound.

56
00:04:10,429 --> 00:04:13,587
Five x over x squared plus one goes to
zero.

57
00:04:14,733 --> 00:04:21,078
e to the negative x approaches zero as x
goes to infinity. e to the x grows faster

58
00:04:21,078 --> 00:04:22,626
than any polynomial.

59
00:04:23,076 --> 00:04:26,596
So x to the tenth over e to the x
approaches zero.

60
00:04:27,733 --> 00:04:31,871
A finite limit at infinity means a
horizontal asymptote.

61
00:04:32,321 --> 00:04:38,302
The logistic function one hundred over one
plus e to the negative x approaches one

62
00:04:38,302 --> 00:04:41,729
hundred: y equals one hundred is its
asymptote.

63
00:04:42,866 --> 00:04:49,383
Your turn. Find the limit as x approaches
infinity of four x cubed minus x over two

64
00:04:49,383 --> 00:04:50,482
minus x cubed.

65
00:04:50,932 --> 00:04:52,468
Pause the video and try it.

66
00:04:52,918 --> 00:04:56,907
Same degree: four over negative one, which
is negative four.

67
00:04:58,033 --> 00:05:03,707
In this last lesson of pre-calculus, we'll
use limits to find the slope of a curve at

68
00:05:03,707 --> 00:05:06,844
a single point. That's the doorway to
calculus.

69
00:05:07,966 --> 00:05:13,087
A secant line through two points on a
curve has a slope we can compute. Slide

70
00:05:13,087 --> 00:05:15,348
the second point toward the first.

71
00:05:15,798 --> 00:05:20,660
The secant lines approach the tangent
line, the line that just touches the curve

72
00:05:20,660 --> 00:05:21,024
there.

73
00:05:22,166 --> 00:05:28,460
The slope of the secant from a to a plus h
is f of a plus h minus f of a, over h.

74
00:05:28,910 --> 00:05:32,878
The tangent slope is the limit of that as
h approaches zero.

75
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:40,114
For f of x equals x squared at a equals
three: three plus h squared minus nine,

76
00:05:40,114 --> 00:05:40,656
over h.

77
00:05:41,106 --> 00:05:47,682
That's six h plus h squared over h, which
is six plus h. As h goes to zero, the

78
00:05:47,682 --> 00:05:48,764
slope is six.

79
00:05:49,900 --> 00:05:53,420
The tangent line passes through three,
nine with slope six.

80
00:05:53,870 --> 00:06:00,312
y minus nine equals six times x minus
three, or y equals six x minus nine.

81
00:06:01,433 --> 00:06:06,340
If s of t is position, the same limit
gives instantaneous velocity.

82
00:06:06,790 --> 00:06:12,938
A ball's height is s of t equals negative
sixteen t squared plus sixty four t. At t

83
00:06:12,938 --> 00:06:18,717
equals one, the limit gives thirty two
feet per second. That's the derivative,

84
00:06:18,717 --> 00:06:20,272
the star of calculus.

85
00:06:21,400 --> 00:06:26,882
Your turn. Find the slope of y equals x
squared at x equals negative two.

86
00:06:27,332 --> 00:06:28,868
Pause the video and try it.

87
00:06:29,318 --> 00:06:32,689
The pattern gives two a, so the slope is
negative four.

88
